@page "/privacy"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Privacy Policy - Blocwerk</PageTitle>

<div class="legal-page">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

    <p>Last updated: 3 September 2026</p>

    <p>
        This policy describes what Blocwerk stores about you, why, and what you can do about it. It
        is written against what this installation of the software actually does &mdash; not a generic
        template.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
        The controller for the processing described here (Art. 4(7) GDPR / DSGVO) is:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Patrick W.</li>
        <li>E-mail: info@blocwerk.app</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Data protection contact: the adress above.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data lives</h2>
    <p>
        Blocwerk is self-hosted. Your data is stored in a PostgreSQL database and in file storage on
        servers operated by ionos. Wall photos, panel photos and beta
        video clips are held as files on that server's disk (older wall photos and clips may still
        sit inside the database itself); avatars are stored in the database.
    </p>

    <h2>Your account and how you sign in</h2>
    <p>Depending on how you sign in, we store some or all of:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Your display name and a provider account identifier</strong>, received from the
            OAuth provider you signed in with. The providers this installation can offer are GitHub,
            Google and Microsoft; only those the operator has configured are actually available.
            Each linked login is stored as a separate record, so you can attach more than one.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A display name you choose yourself</strong>, which overrides the one from the
            provider.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>An e-mail address</strong>, if you set one. It is stored only after you confirm
            it with a code we e-mail you, and it is used for account recovery and for the sign-up and
            e-mail-verification flows. Verification codes are stored only as hashes, expire after ten
            minutes, and are invalidated once used.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A login username and password</strong>, if you set one. The password is never
            stored: only a salted key-derivation hash of it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A two-factor (TOTP) secret</strong>, if you enable an authenticator app. It is
            encrypted at rest and is never displayed again after enrolment.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A failed-attempt counter and lockout time</strong>, so that repeated wrong
            passwords or codes lock the account for a short period. This is a security measure
            (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
        </li>
        <li><strong>An avatar image</strong>, if you upload one.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Preferences</strong>: your home wall, preferred grade scale, progression window
            and grouping.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Legal basis: performing the service you asked for (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR) for everything needed
        to run your account, and our legitimate interest in a secure service (Art. 6(1)(f)) for the
        anti-brute-force counters.
    </p>

    <h2>What you do in the app</h2>
    <p>Using Blocwerk creates records tied to your account:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Boulders you create, and setter credits naming you as a setter or co-setter</li>
        <li>Attempts you log (attempt, send, flash), with time and optional notes</li>
        <li>Comments, star ratings, grade proposals and favourites</li>
        <li>Live climbing sessions, hangboard sessions and pull-up sessions</li>
        <li>
            "Activities" &mdash; clusters of the above grouped by time, used for the progression
            charts &mdash; and their duration if you edit it
        </li>
        <li>Beta video clips you upload, along with a poster frame taken from them</li>
        <li>
            A per-wall activity log recording who created, revised, reset or re-photographed what,
            and when
        </li>
        <li>Your membership of each wall and your role on it (member, moderator, admin, owner)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Some of this is visible to other members of the walls you belong to: your name and avatar,
        your boulders and setter credits, your comments and ratings, your logged sends on a wall
        leaderboard, and the wall activity log. A wall admin can also generate an anonymous share
        link that lets someone without an account view that wall and its activity.
    </p>

    <h2>Photos of walls and panels</h2>
    <p>
        Wall and panel photos are uploaded by wall admins and processed on the server to detect
        holds. They are pictures of a climbing wall, but people can end up in them. If you appear in
        a wall photo and want it removed, contact the wall's admin or us at [[CONTACT EMAIL]].
    </p>

    <h2>Kiosk mode (the tablet at the wall)</h2>
    <p>
        A wall admin can register a shared device &mdash; typically a tablet mounted at the wall
        &mdash; as a "kiosk". This matters for your privacy, so it works like this:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            You only appear on a kiosk's member picker if you have <strong>explicitly consented</strong>
            for that wall. Consent is stored per wall membership, so it never carries over to your
            other walls, and you can withdraw it at any time.
        </li>
        <li>
            You may set a short <strong>PIN</strong> that must be typed before anyone can pick you at
            the tablet. Only a salted hash of the PIN is stored, plus its length so the tablet knows
            when an entry is complete.
        </li>
        <li>
            While someone has picked you at the tablet, anything they log &mdash; attempts, sends,
            comments, boulders &mdash; is recorded <strong>as you</strong>, exactly as if you had
            logged it on your own phone. The session ends after 30 minutes of inactivity or when
            released.
        </li>
        <li>
            A kiosk session cannot change your password, second factor or e-mail, cannot link another
            login to your account, cannot mint API keys, and <strong>cannot delete your account</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
            The tablet is identified by a long-lived <code>blocwerk-kiosk</code> device cookie. It
            identifies the device, not a person.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A wall admin can additionally allow <strong>anonymous setting</strong> at that wall's kiosk:
        somebody standing at the tablet can then record a new boulder without anybody being signed
        in at all. Those boulders are credited to <strong>Ghost</strong> &mdash; a placeholder
        account that is nobody, the same name a deleted account's content is shown under &mdash; and
        no personal data whatsoever is recorded for them. The wall admin can turn this off again at
        any time.
    </p>

    <h2>API keys</h2>
    <p>
        You can create personal API keys (they begin <code>bwk_</code>) so a script or device can act
        as you against the Blocwerk REST API. Wall admins can create wall- and kiosk-scoped keys used
        by mounted tablets and by sensors that post wall temperature readings. Only a SHA-256 hash of
        each key is stored, along with its name, a short display prefix, creation and expiry time,
        and a throttled "last used" timestamp. Revoke a key you no longer need.
    </p>

    <h2>Importing from TopLogger</h2>
    <p>
        If you connect a TopLogger account, we store your TopLogger access and refresh tokens
        <strong>encrypted at rest</strong>, your TopLogger user id, the time of the last sync and any
        sync error. We then fetch and store your ascents from that service (climb name, gym, date,
        grade, points) so they appear alongside your Blocwerk climbs. This is optional, based on your
        consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR), and disconnecting removes the connection.
        <strong>TopLogger is a third party with its own privacy policy</strong>; connecting sends
        requests to their servers.
    </p>

    <h2>Sending e-mail</h2>
    <p>
        Verification, sign-up and password-reset codes are sent through an SMTP server configured by
        the operator: info@blocwerk.app via ionos. Your address and the message are processed by that
        provider in the course of delivery.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies and local storage</h2>
    <p>
        Blocwerk sets no advertising or tracking cookies and uses no third-party analytics. The
        cookies it does set are:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Authentication cookie</strong> &mdash; keeps you signed in; expires after 8 hours of inactivity</li>
        <li><strong>Antiforgery cookie</strong> &mdash; protects form submissions against cross-site request forgery</li>
        <li><strong><code>cookie_consent</code></strong> &mdash; remembers that you acknowledged this notice</li>
        <li><strong><code>bw_totp_pending</code></strong> &mdash; short-lived, carries a pending two-factor challenge between steps</li>
        <li><strong><code>bw_link_intent</code></strong> &mdash; short-lived, carries an account-linking request through the OAuth round trip</li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>bw_login_method</code>, <code>bw_method_counts</code>,
            <code>bw_method_optout</code>, <code>blocwerk-returning</code></strong> &mdash; remember
            which sign-in method you use so the login page can offer it first. You can opt out of
            this on the sign-in page.
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>blocwerk-kiosk</code></strong> &mdash; marks a device as a registered kiosk tablet (see above)</li>
        <li><strong><code>blocwerk-last-page</code>, <code>blocwerk-zoom-lens-mag</code></strong> &mdash; interface preferences the server reads on first paint</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Your browser additionally keeps, on your device only and never sent to us: theme and layout
        preferences, the wall-dimming and focus settings, the collapsed state of the navigation bar,
        the app-install prompt state, and &mdash; when you are offline &mdash; an IndexedDB queue of
        actions (logged attempts, comments, boulders) waiting to be sent when you reconnect.
    </p>

    <h2>Server logs and metrics</h2>
    <p>
        The application exposes operational metrics (request counts and timings, and totals such as
        the number of walls, boulders and registered users). These are aggregate figures and are not
        labelled with your identity. Application logs may record user and wall identifiers alongside
        errors and administrative actions, and the web server may record IP addresses and request
        paths in the usual way. Log retention: 7 days.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep things</h2>
    <p>
        Account data is kept for as long as your account exists. Content you contributed to a wall is
        kept for as long as that wall exists (see the next section for what happens when you delete
        your account). Backups: 30 days. Other retention periods: as required by law.
    </p>

    <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
    <p>
        You can erase your account yourself, at any time, under
        <a href="/settings/delete-account">Profile &rarr; Delete account</a>. It is irreversible and
        it is not available from a kiosk tablet.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Deleting your account permanently erases:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Your name, chosen display name, e-mail address and avatar image</li>
        <li>Your password, your two-factor secret and every linked GitHub / Google / Microsoft login</li>
        <li>
            Your sign-in tokens and your account's pending verification codes, and your personal
            API keys. Any session still open elsewhere &mdash; another browser, another device
            &mdash; stops working at once: an erased account can no longer be signed in as, and the
            browser is signed out the next time it asks.
        </li>
        <li>Your TopLogger connection, its stored tokens and every ascent imported from it</li>
        <li>Your hangboard, pull-up and climbing sessions, your training activities, and your favourites</li>
        <li>Your wall memberships, and with them your kiosk consent and kiosk PIN</li>
        <li>Beta video clips you uploaded, including the files themselves</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>It deliberately does not erase</strong> the content other people's walls are built on:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Boulders you created and setter credits naming you</li>
        <li>Comments, star ratings and grade proposals you wrote</li>
        <li>Attempts you logged, so the send counts on shared boulders do not change</li>
        <li>Wall history entries (resets, photo updates) on walls you helped run</li>
        <li>
            Wall- and kiosk-scoped API keys you created. These are <strong>transferred</strong> to
            the wall's owner rather than deleted, because they are what the gym's tablet and sensors
            authenticate with and deleting them would take that hardware offline. The name you gave
            each key is replaced with a neutral one; the key itself keeps working under its new
            owner, who can revoke it.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Your user record is stripped of every personal field and left as an anonymous placeholder
        shown as <strong>Ghost</strong>, and the free text you typed that was only ever about you
        &mdash; the notes on your logged attempts, and any text you entered that was copied into the
        wall activity log &mdash; is blanked. Deleting the rest instead would tear holes in walls
        other members depend on.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Be aware of what that leaves.</strong> The words you wrote stay: the names you gave
        your boulders, the text of your comments, your grade proposals. If any of it names you or
        describes you, it is still there after the deletion, credited to Ghost. We cannot review it
        for you &mdash; so if you want a specific boulder, comment or photo removed as well, ask us
        at info@blocwerk.app before you delete your account.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you are the <strong>only owner of a wall</strong> and no other admin can take it over,
        deletion is refused until you make somebody else an admin or delete the wall &mdash; so a
        wall full of other people's climbing is never left ownerless or silently destroyed.
        Otherwise the wall passes to its longest-standing remaining admin.
    </p>
    <p>
        Deletion cannot reach copies that live outside the live database: <strong>backups</strong>
        (removed on the schedule above), <strong>server and application logs</strong>, and anything
        already sent to a third party such as your OAuth provider, the e-mail provider or TopLogger.
        Those must be dealt with at their source.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>Under the GDPR/DSGVO you have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Access</strong> (Art. 15) &mdash; a copy of the data we hold about you</li>
        <li><strong>Rectification</strong> (Art. 16) &mdash; correction of inaccurate data; you can edit your name, e-mail and avatar yourself</li>
        <li><strong>Erasure</strong> (Art. 17) &mdash; deletion, as described above and available self-service</li>
        <li><strong>Restriction</strong> (Art. 18) and <strong>objection</strong> (Art. 21) to processing based on legitimate interests</li>
        <li><strong>Data portability</strong> (Art. 20) &mdash; your own records in a machine-readable form; your personal API keys already give you programmatic access to your climbing data</li>
        <li><strong>Withdraw consent</strong> (Art. 7(3)) at any time, e.g. kiosk consent or the TopLogger connection, without affecting past processing</li>
        <li><strong>Complain to a supervisory authority</strong> (Art. 77): [[SUPERVISORY AUTHORITY]]</li>
    </ul>
    <p>To exercise any of these, write to info@blocwerk.app.</p>

    <h2>Third parties</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>GitHub, Google, Microsoft</strong> &mdash; whichever OAuth sign-in you use</li>
        <li><strong>[[E-MAIL / SMTP PROVIDER]]</strong> &mdash; delivery of verification and reset e-mails</li>
        <li><strong>TopLogger</strong> &mdash; only if you connect it</li>
        <li><strong>[[HOSTING PROVIDER]]</strong> &mdash; the servers this installation runs on</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        No data is sold, and none is shared with advertisers or analytics services.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
        If this policy changes materially we will update the date at the top and, where the change
        affects you, tell you in the app.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Patrick W., info@blocwerk.app</p>
</div>
